(function(){'use strict';const lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;const changes={"jasmine-after-rain":{"avatar-12":{"number":12,"title":"The Dragon's Breath","content":"The dragon's breath was the name of a tea Iroh served only when he had made a difficult decision.\n\nIt was black, smoky, and strong enough to make my eyes water. He poured it into two cups after the morning rush, when the shop had finally quieted.\n\n“This seems hostile,” I said.\n\n“It is honest.”\n\n“You keep using that word to describe things that taste like ash.”\n\n“Some truths are not sweet.”\n\nI lifted the cup. “What did you decide?”\n\nIroh sat across from me. The letter from the Fire Nation lay unopened beside the tea.\n\n“I will go,” he said.\n\nThe room seemed to tilt.\n\n“For how long?”\n\n“Three weeks at first. Perhaps longer if the negotiations require it.”\n\n“Where?”\n\n“The western provinces. Then the capital.”\n\nI held the cup between my hands. The heat was almost painful.\n\n“You don’t have to look so calm,” Iroh said.\n\n“I’m trying not to make this about me.”\n\n“It is about you. It is also about many other things.”\n\n“You cannot be responsible for all of them.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Do you?”\n\nHis mouth tightened. “I am learning.”\n\nI took a sip of the tea and immediately regretted it.\n\nIroh's eyes warmed. “You need not finish it.”\n\n“I am committed to the lesson.”\n\n“Pride is a poor seasoning.”\n\n“You made the tea.”\n\n“And I am willing to admit it is unpleasant.”\n\nI laughed despite the ache in my chest.\n\nIroh reached across the table. “I will write.”\n\n“Letters?”\n\n“Letters.”\n\n“Not just formal reports.”\n\n“I promise to include at least one complaint about the food.”\n\n“And one thing you are actually feeling.”\n\nHe hesitated.\n\n“Iroh.”\n\n“I will try.”\n\n“That is not the same as promise.”\n\n“I promise to try honestly.”\n\nI accepted that.\n\nThe days before his departure became a strange mixture of work and preparation. Iroh arranged for another tea master to oversee the shop. I learned which suppliers were reliable and which ones sold leaves that had been stored too near fish.\n\nMina found the revised rota on the counter and read it twice.\n\n\"Who put me on closing duty every night?\" she asked.\n\nIroh looked up from his travel papers. \"I thought the extra wages might be welcome.\"\n\n\"Wages are welcome. Seven late shifts in a row are not.\"\n\nHe folded the paper. That was when I realized he'd planned a tea shop with the efficiency of a former general and the imagination of a man who did not have to stay behind and scrub its kettles.\n\n\"How many people have agreed to this?\" Mina asked.\n\n\"Not enough,\" I said.\n\nIroh reached for a pen, then stopped. \"I was going to ask when the plan was finished.\"\n\n\"You can ask before you finish,\" she said. \"It saves ink.\"\n\nShe crossed out four evenings and penciled in the other tea master's actual hours. She could handle the accounts on Tuesday, but not a delivery at dawn as well. Iroh would have to find a runner or shorten the menu.\n\n\"Shorten the menu?\" He sounded deeply offended.\n\n\"The world will survive without the smoked plum blend for three days.\"\n\n\"I am less certain I will.\"\n\nWhen Mina returned to the front room, he looked at the rota for a long time. His finger rested on the blank lines.\n\n\"This isn't an army,\" I said.\n\nThe words came out too sharply, and his smile disappeared. I didn't take them back.\n\n\"No,\" he said. \"And this is not a siege to be won by careful arrangements made over other people's heads.\"\n\nHe rewrote the schedule with empty spaces where workers could choose their shifts. Then he sealed the first of his letters to the Fire Nation. It took him longer than he'd spent drawing the route, perhaps because this time the people whose lives he was arranging could speak back.\n\n“Would you oversee the accounts while I am gone?” Iroh asked.\n\n“That sounds like an error.”\n\n“It is a calculated risk.”\n\n“You have known me for less than a month.”\n\n“I have known you for long enough to see that you notice what others overlook.”\n\n“You are going to make me responsible for the kettle.”\n\n“Only the kettle with a temper.”\n\n“You are leaving me with your most difficult employee.”\n\n“You are the most difficult employee.”\n\n“I have never threatened to whistle at you.”\n\n“The kettle has never threatened to leave.”\n\nThe joke faded between us.\n\nOn the last evening, I climbed to the balcony with two cups of tea. The city stretched below, lanterns appearing one by one.\n\n“Are you afraid?” Iroh asked.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Of what?”\n\n“That you’ll realize the world needs you more than I do.”\n\n“The world needs many things. It does not decide whom I love.”\n\n“You could meet someone.”\n\n“I could.”\n\nI looked at him.\n\n“That was not helpful,” he admitted.\n\n“No.”\n\n“But it is true that I cannot promise nothing will ever change. I can promise that I will not make decisions about us in silence.”\n\n“You will write.”\n\n“I will write.”\n\n“You will tell me when you are lonely.”\n\n“I will tell you.”\n\n“You will not turn yourself into a weapon just because people expect it.”\n\nIroh looked toward the city wall. “I will try.”\n\n“Honestly.”\n\n“Honestly.”\n\nI sat beside him. The balcony railing was cool under my hand.\n\n“What do you want to be when you come back?” I asked.\n\nHe thought about it.\n\n“The man who owns a tea shop,” he said. “The man who knows which blend you prefer. The man who has a room above the shop and someone who complains about his breakfast.”\n\n“You do not have to become smaller to be loved.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You can be both.”\n\n“Both what?”\n\n“The person who helps, and the person who rests. The man people call when they need wisdom, and the man who wants to sleep in late.”\n\nIroh smiled. “I would like to sleep in late.”\n\n“You can start when you return.”\n\n“That is a generous offer.”\n\n“Do not mistake it for permission to stop making tea.”\n\nHe laughed, then leaned toward me.\n\n“May I kiss you?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nWe shared a quiet kiss, letting the coming separation sit between us without turning it into a promise either of us could not keep.\n\nHe paused when my fingers tightened in his coat.\n\n“Still comfortable?”\n\n“I am sad.”\n\n“That was not my question.”\n\n“Yes. I am comfortable.”\n\nHe kissed me again, more deeply. The city lights trembled below. When the cold finally drove us inside, neither of us pretended the leaving had become easier.\n\nWe stayed at the table until the tea went cold, talking through travel routes, people he meant to find, and all the practical details that were easier to name than goodbye. Every so often one of us made a joke and the other let it work for a minute. It was not enough to make the separation harmless. It was enough to keep it human.\n\nBefore dawn, Iroh left a letter beneath my cup.\n\nIt contained only one sentence: I WILL COME BACK TO THE TEA.\n\nI folded it carefully and put it beside my bed.\n\nThe morning after his decision, Iroh woke before me and tried to move quietly. He failed when the floorboard beside the bed complained.\n\n“The room is betraying me,” he said.\n\nI opened one eye. “The room has always been honest.”\n\n“It has chosen a poor time.”\n\nHe sat on the edge of the bed. His travel bag rested near the door. Seeing it made the room feel smaller.\n\n“You are allowed to change your mind,” I said.\n\n“About leaving?”\n\n“About anything.”\n\nIroh looked at the bag. “I know. That is why the choice is difficult.”\n\n“If you stay, you may be accused of selfishness.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“If you go, you may be accused of abandoning me.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“People are going to accuse you either way.”\n\nHe smiled faintly. “You have found the center of the problem.”\n\nI sat up. “Then choose what you can live with.”\n\n“I am choosing to help people who have no other advocate.”\n\n“And you are choosing to come back.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Then I will trust the choices, not the distance between them.”\n\nIroh's hand found mine.\n\n“You are making this easier,” he said.\n\n“I am not trying to.”\n\n“That is why it helps.”\n\nThat afternoon Mina found a second problem in the arrangements: the supplier would not deliver the fresh leaves without Iroh's signature, and the substitute tea master had never been authorized to sign for him.\n\n“Then the deliveries will have to wait,” Iroh said.\n\n“The customers won't,” Mina replied. “And neither will the farmers who have already picked the leaves.”\n\nHe was quiet for a moment. I could see him planning a route around the inconvenience as though the people involved were stones to be moved on a Pai Sho board. Then he took the paper back.\n\n“Would you accept responsibility for receiving them?” he asked Mina.\n\n“If I can reject spoiled stock, yes. If you expect me to pay a shortfall out of my wages, absolutely not.”\n\n“Of course not.”\n\n“Write that down.”\n\nHe did. He also wrote that the price negotiated with the growers could not be lowered without their agreement, even if the shipment arrived late. I held the counter steady while Mina signed the new authority sheet. No one thanked her for being generous. It was her work, and she was being trusted to do it.\n\nIroh folded his travel letter beside the agreement, then looked at me.\n\n“I am accustomed to issuing orders before departure.”\n\n“I noticed.”\n\n“An unfortunate habit in a man who has spent so long saying he wishes to retire.”\n\n“You can still ask for help.”\n\nHe glanced at Mina, who was already checking the delivery shelf. “It appears I must learn to ask before arranging the answer.”\n\n\nDownstairs, the shop needed to be prepared for his absence. I wrote instructions in his careful handwriting because my own letters were too large for the narrow page. The substitute tea master complained about the kettle. I told her that the kettle was now her responsibility.\n\nShe looked horrified.\n\n“It is only a kettle,” I said.\n\n“That is what he said before it attacked him.”\n\nIroh packed a second letter for Zuko. He folded it three times before sealing it.\n\n“Will he be angry?” I asked.\n\n“Possibly.”\n\n“Will he understand?”\n\n“Eventually.”\n\n“You sound certain.”\n\n“I am certain he loves me. Understanding may take longer.”\n\nI watched him press the seal into the wax.\n\n“Do you want him to visit the shop?”\n\n“I want him to visit because he wants to, not because I have created another obligation.”\n\n“You worry about that a lot.”\n\n“Obligation can look very much like love when people are frightened.”\n\nI touched his wrist. “I will not stay because I owe you.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“I will not come because I owe you.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“I am coming because I want to see the road.”\n\n“And the governors?”\n\n“I want to see you argue with them.”\n\n“That is a very specific desire.”\n\n“You are impressive when you are angry.”\n\nIroh laughed. “I will try to be impressive responsibly.”\n\nThe day passed in preparations. Customers brought food for the journey. The old pai sho players gave Iroh a tile they claimed was lucky. The woman with the bright scarves left a bundle of dried flowers and told me not to let him forget to sleep.\n\n“Everyone is giving me instructions,” Iroh said.\n\n“Now you know how I feel.”\n\nHe kissed my temple.\n\nThat evening, I made the dragon's breath tea. I did not like it any better, but I understood why he chose it.\n\n“It tastes like a decision,” I said.\n\n“Some decisions are not sweet.”\n\n“You have said that too.”\n\n“It remains true.”\n\nI lifted my cup. “Then to difficult truth.”\n\nIroh touched his cup to mine. “And to returning.”\n\nLater, before departure, we shared one last kiss and sat together until it was time to sleep.\n\nAt dawn, he left the letter beneath my cup.\n\nI carried it against my heart while I walked beside him into the rain.\n\nAt the gate, Iroh stopped and adjusted the strap of my pack.\n\n“You are certain?” he asked.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You may still turn back.”\n\n“Ask me tomorrow.”\n\nHis smile was small and relieved.\n\nTogether, Iroh and I stepped onto the road.\n\nBehind me, the tea shop waited with its lamps dark and its door locked. Ahead, the rain softened the distance.\n\nIroh kept my hand in his.\n\nThe rain continued around me, gentle enough to endure.\n\nThe city disappeared behind the mist.\n\nThe road opened ahead.\n\nIroh adjusted the pack on his shoulder and offered me his hand. I took it without hesitation.","word_count":2121},"avatar-25":{"number":25,"title":"The Garden Behind the Shop","content":"The vine had to be moved before it climbed into the kitchen window.\n\nIroh called it an old medicinal variety. Mina called it a threat to the shutters. I called it the thing that had wound itself around the drainpipe in a single hot week.\n\n\"Perhaps it likes our hospitality,\" Iroh said.\n\n\"Perhaps it wants to eat the shop.\"\n\nHe considered this with insulting seriousness. \"Then we must charge it rent.\"\n\nWe had promised ourselves an afternoon in the garden, and I had pictured a little watering and maybe tea beneath the shade cloth. Instead, the top bed had turned to mud, the jasmine leaves were yellow at their edges, and a leak in the watering line had been running since breakfast.\n\nLian, the neighbor whose wall bordered the garden, leaned over the low partition.\n\n\"You're watering my bricks,\" she said.\n\n\"We're sorry,\" I said.\n\n\"You said that yesterday.\"\n\n\"I said Iroh was sorry yesterday,\" I corrected. \"Today I'm sorry too.\"\n\n\"Progress.\" She pointed to the dark stain spreading beneath her window. \"That mortar won't stay put if it keeps getting soaked.\"\n\nIroh put down the pruning shears. He looked at the wall, then at the vine. \"I have misjudged several things.\"\n\n\"An unusually productive afternoon,\" I said.\n\nWe turned off the garden tap. Mina came out with the stock book tucked under one arm, because the shop's water wasn't an endless miracle either. She showed us the weekly delivery costs. Replacing the ruined irrigation hose would mean postponing a set of decorative jars Iroh had wanted for the front shelf.\n\n\"The jars are charming,\" he said.\n\n\"So are walls that don't collapse,\" Mina answered.\n\nLian offered a length of spare hose from her own repairs on the condition that we return the clamp next week. I wrote the loan down. It was easier to accept a favor when it had a name and a return date, instead of becoming the sort of debt everybody felt awkward mentioning.\n\nWe dug a narrow channel to let the wet soil drain away from the shared wall. Iroh knelt in the dirt beside me. His sleeves were rolled up, his hands as practiced at pulling weeds as they had once been at something far less gentle.\n\n\"How do you know it's medicinal?\" I asked, pointing at the vine.\n\n\"An old acquaintance told me.\"\n\n\"Did he have a name?\"\n\n\"Several, depending on who was asking.\"\n\n\"That inspires confidence.\"\n\nHe plucked a leaf, frowned at its shape, and held it toward his nose. I caught his wrist.\n\n\"No tasting mystery plants.\"\n\n\"An outrageous accusation. I was going to smell it.\"\n\n\"You were absolutely going to taste it.\"\n\n\"Only a very small amount.\"\n\n\"Mina!\" I called.\n\n\"Do not let him eat the plant!\" she called back, without appearing.\n\nI laughed hard enough to drop the trowel. Iroh joined in. It surprised me how good laughter could feel when the day's trouble was still sitting in plain view.\n\nWe asked a local gardener to identify the vine rather than pretending the first confident story was the correct one. It wasn't the tea herb Iroh thought it was. It could stay for shade if we pruned it, but it did not belong in any cup. Mina wrote NOT TEA on a scrap of card and tied it to the trellis.\n\nIroh regarded the label with wounded dignity. \"It has other talents.\"\n\n\"So do you. I still don't put you in the kettle.\"\n\nLian came through the garden gate before the tools were put away. Her sleeves were damp, and she held a tray of seedlings whose roots had nearly drowned beneath our broken pipe.\n\n\"I don't want a speech,\" she said. \"I want to know whether the leak is fixed.\"\n\nIroh bowed. \"It is. And I shall replace everything that was lost.\"\n\n\"Not everything was lost.\" She pointed to a corner of the tray. \"Those will recover. The rest can be bought from Huan at the next market.\"\n\nI appreciated that she didn't let him turn her damaged plants into a grand gesture. We wrote the replacements down, including the price Huan would charge and who would collect them. Mina added the amount to the actual shop accounts rather than treating it as another free favor expected of her.\n\nIroh examined the list. \"It appears I have purchased a garden lesson at considerable expense.\"\n\n\"You also purchased an afternoon of wrestling a vine,\" I said.\n\n\"Which I enjoyed until it attempted to conquer the drainpipe.\"\n\nLian smiled. \"Then perhaps next time you'll ask before planting something beside my wall.\"\n\n\"I will.\"\n\nBefore Lian left, Iroh asked if she wanted the damaged corner of the garden replanted with the same vine.\n\n“No,” she said. “I want the soil tested before you put anything else against that wall.”\n\nThe answer surprised him. He had spent the afternoon making the space look as though nothing had happened, but he hadn't thought to ask whether restoring the appearance was what she needed.\n\n“What would you plant there?” I asked her.\n\n“I don't know yet. The roots are still wet. Huan has three varieties that tolerate shade, but I need to find out which will survive without forcing the drain to do the same work again.”\n\nIroh nodded. “Then the garden must wait for your answer.”\n\n“Good.” Lian glanced at the trellis. “And the vine belongs on your side. You can love it as much as you like from there.”\n\nHe looked at the tangled leaves with reluctant affection. “I may have been a poor neighbor.”\n\n“Very poor,” she said. “But you're getting better at asking.”\n\nShe left us the list of plants that could be saved and the address of the grower she trusted. Iroh put the invoice in the shop ledger, not his private account of romantic gestures. He drew a small square around the disputed garden strip and wrote *no planting until Lian approves*.\n\nI kissed his muddy cheek. He looked down at me with a mixture of surprise and delight.\n\n“What was that for?”\n\n“For not trying to win a gardening war.”\n\n“A devastating concession. I had prepared several strategies.”\n\n\nShe left with her tray, and the evening breeze moved through the leaves above us. I could smell wet earth and the jasmine that had survived our clumsy attempts at help. Iroh took my muddy hand in both of his.\n\n\"You were right to stop me from eating that leaf,\" he said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"An admission that cost you nothing.\"\n\n\"You were about to poison yourself for a philosophical point.\"\n\n\"For botanical research.\"\n\n\"You're incorrigible.\"\n\nHe looked almost shy. \"May I kiss the person who keeps me from disastrous research?\"\n\nI stepped closer. The kiss was warm, gentle, and interrupted by Mina calling down that our tea had gone cold. I laughed against his cheek.\n\nWe sat on the garden bench until the shadows covered the trellis. I told him about a visit I'd arranged with people I loved elsewhere in Lanternweald. He listened, asked when I hoped to return, and didn't make me justify the relationships we'd already spoken about honestly.\n\n\"I prefer polyamory, Iroh,\" I reminded him. \"I'm ethically non-monogamous. I also like this garden, even when it tries to eat the building.\"\n\n\"Then I shall reserve you a place to sit,\" he said, \"not a place you must occupy.\"\n\nHe wrote *Garden bench, Wednesday, if Jasper wishes* on the calendar. I crossed out the last four words and replaced them with *Ask Jasper*. We both laughed.\n\nThe shop still needed tomorrow's deliveries. Lian still needed seedlings. Our evening didn't solve either problem, but we could kiss goodnight without pretending that affection was payment for finishing the work.\n\nBy late afternoon, the drainpipe was clear. The jasmine had been moved out of the hottest sun, the hose patched, and Lian's wall no longer glistened with fresh water. We had a list of things to check in the morning, because fixing a leak once didn't mean we could forget about it.\n\nMina signed off the day's stock before she went home. Iroh thanked her, and this time he did not offer to replace wages with compliments.\n\nI carried the basin to the upper room while he washed the dirt from his hands. His knuckles were scraped from the brickwork. I reached for a clean cloth.\n\n\"May I?\" I asked.\n\nHe nodded. I dabbed the worst of the scrape. His fingers curled around mine.\n\n\"You were very firm with me today,\" he said.\n\n\"You were trying to taste the vine.\"\n\n\"I was also trying to be useful.\"\n\n\"You were useful. Then you were wrong. You can do both.\"\n\nHe leaned his forehead against mine. \"I am still learning to be loved by someone who argues.\"\n\n\"You're dating me. That's the entire experience.\"\n\nA smile pulled at the corner of his mouth.\n\nWe had already talked about my other relationships, about how I prefer polyamory and honesty over exclusivity, and about the fact that affection didn't erase anybody's need for a quiet evening. I told him I was staying tonight because I wanted to, not because he had earned it by repairing a pipe.\n\n\"Then may I kiss you without submitting a maintenance report?\" he asked.\n\n\"One report. In triplicate.\"\n\nHe kissed my fingertips first, then my mouth, slow enough that I could choose to move closer. Through the open window we could hear Mina laughing with Lian about the sign on the vine.\n\nThe shop would open in the morning. The trellis would need trimming again before long. We closed the curtains and went to bed, leaving the list where both of us could find it.","word_count":1607},"avatar-47":{"number":47,"title":"The Empty Chair Is Not a Threat","content":"The chair beside the window had been empty for twelve days.\n\nI knew because Mara had asked whether the teahouse needed another delivery of bread, and Iroh had said, \"Only if Mr. Halen returns.\" He said it lightly, but I noticed his eyes move toward the small table where the old man usually sat.\n\nHalen was not an especially cheerful customer. He came three mornings a week, drank one cup of the house blend, complained about the weather, and left two coins on the table even when the price was lower. He had once informed me that my sweeping technique lacked discipline.\n\nI liked him.\n\nWhen he stopped coming, I assumed he had found another shop.\n\nIroh did not.\n\n\"Would you like to check on him?\" I asked.\n\n\"I would,\" he said. \"But I do not wish to make his absence into an accusation.\"\n\n\"Then we can ask whether he's well. He can tell us to go away.\"\n\nIroh considered that.\n\n\"An excellent outcome, if he is well enough to do so.\"\n\nWe found the address through the delivery notes Halen had voluntarily given the teahouse for winter weather. Iroh checked that the contact permission included welfare visits, not just tea delivery. It did.\n\nHe wrapped a small packet of bread and tea leaves, and we walked up Jasmine Steps toward the narrow houses behind the herb gardens.\n\nThe path was damp. Water ran through the channels between the stones, carrying the faint smell of rosemary. At Halen's gate, a blue flowerpot lay on its side.\n\nIroh knocked.\n\nNothing happened.\n\nHe knocked again, then stepped back.\n\nA window opened above us.\n\n\"Go away,\" Halen called.\n\nI looked at Iroh.\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"Good morning, Mr. Halen. We noticed you have not visited. We wished to know whether you are well.\"\n\n\"I'm alive.\"\n\n\"An encouraging beginning.\"\n\n\"I don't want tea.\"\n\n\"Then we shall not insist.\"\n\nThe window began to close.\n\nI spoke before I could stop myself.\n\n\"Do you need help with the flowerpot?\"\n\nThere was a pause.\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"The one by the gate. It's fallen over.\"\n\nHalen looked down.\n\n\"Blasted wind.\"\n\n\"Would you like me to set it upright?\"\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"Yes. Don't break it.\"\n\nI picked up the pot and found a crack along the base. The soil had spilled into the path, and the plant's roots were exposed.\n\n\"This needs a new pot,\" I said.\n\nHalen sighed.\n\n\"Of course it does.\"\n\nIroh looked up at him.\n\n\"May we come in with it?\"\n\nA long silence followed.\n\nThen we heard the stairs.\n\nHalen opened the door wearing a coat over his nightclothes. His hair was uncombed. The house behind him smelled of dust and old paper.\n\n\"Five minutes,\" he said.\n\nWe went inside.\n\nThe room was filled with boxes. On the table lay a bundle of letters tied with blue string. Halen noticed me looking and put a hand over them.\n\n\"Private.\"\n\n\"Of course,\" I said.\n\nI turned away.\n\nWe found a spare pot in the kitchen and moved the plant into it. Iroh made tea only after Halen asked for some. The bread stayed wrapped on the counter until he decided whether he wanted it.\n\nFor the first ten minutes we talked about weather and soil.\n\nThen Halen sat down.\n\n\"My sister died,\" he said.\n\nI felt the words more than heard them.\n\nIroh placed his cup on the table.\n\n\"I am sorry.\"\n\nHalen nodded.\n\n\"She used to write to me every week. We argued about everything. The proper way to prune roses. Whether the sea-glass orchard was a waste of good stone. Whether I should stop eating so much bread.\"\n\nI looked at the bundle of letters.\n\nHe followed my glance.\n\n\"I don't want anyone to read them.\"\n\n\"Then nobody will,\" I said.\n\nHalen stared at the table.\n\n\"I stopped coming to the teahouse because she liked it. She always asked whether I had seen the old general who served jasmine tea. I would tell her he was overbearing.\"\n\nIroh smiled faintly.\n\n\"An accurate assessment.\"\n\nHalen's mouth twitched.\n\n\"I didn't want to sit in the chair and think about how I couldn't tell her it was raining.\"\n\nHalen touched the edge of his empty cup.\n\n\"She hated this blend,\" he said. \"Always told me it tasted like somebody had brewed the rain.\"\n\nIroh laughed softly. \"A severe but memorable criticism.\"\n\n\"She liked the sound of this room. The cups. The door. People arriving.\"\n\nI thought of the twelve empty mornings and how easily we'd imagined that his absence was a message to us. It had been a grief we couldn't see.\n\n\"Would you like us to send a note when the shop has a quiet afternoon?\" I asked.\n\nHe considered. \"Perhaps. But don't start checking on me every time I miss tea.\"\n\n\"No,\" I said. \"You can come when you want.\"\n\nHe looked surprised by the simplicity of it. Iroh set down the kettle and asked whether he might bring Halen another cup now, or whether he'd prefer to be left alone.\n\n\"Another cup,\" Halen said. \"And I still think the tea is weak.\"\n\nIroh smiled in a way that made my chest ache.\n\nWhen Halen had gone, I found Iroh carefully wiping down the table beside the window. I took the cloth from him only after he held it out.\n\n\"You're thinking of Lu Ten,\" I said.\n\n\"Yes. And of how many people here have stories that don't belong to me.\"\n\nWe stood beside each other for a while. I had told him about my polyamorous relationships and the people I loved elsewhere; he had told me how it sometimes hurt to see an empty chair. Neither truth canceled the other.\n\n\"Would you like some company?\" I asked.\n\nHe looked at the table, then at me. \"I would.\"\n\nWe put away the clean cups together. It was almost closing time. Outside the street vendors were calling their final prices, and nothing about the city's ordinary noise had become less important because one person was grieving.\n\nI understood then why the empty chair mattered. Not because a regular customer had stopped spending coins, but because a small ordinary place had become painful to enter.\n\n\"Do you want us to keep the chair for you?\" I asked.\n\nHalen looked startled.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"All right.\"\n\nHe frowned.\n\n\"You're not going to insist?\"\n\n\"No. You can come back if you want. You can choose a different seat. Or you can stay home.\"\n\nIroh nodded.\n\n\"The table will remain part of the teahouse, but you are not a duty it must fulfill.\"\n\nHalen looked at him for a long moment.\n\n\"You're a strange man.\"\n\n\"Often.\"\n\nHe finally unwrapped the bread.\n\nWe left after half an hour, not because we were unwelcome but because Halen had asked for some quiet. He kept the plant on the windowsill and accepted the tea leaves.\n\nOn the walk back, Iroh was silent.\n\n\"Are you thinking about Lu Ten?\" I asked.\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"Would you like to talk?\"\n\n\"Not today.\"\n\n\"All right.\"\n\nHe looked grateful for the simplicity of the answer.\n\nAt the teahouse, the chair by the window remained empty. We cleaned it like every other chair and set the day's menu on the table. Iroh did not leave a cup waiting. I did not watch the door every few minutes.\n\nA week later, Halen returned.\n\nHe walked in carrying the repaired flowerpot and set it on the counter.\n\n\"Your tea was too strong,\" he told Iroh.\n\n\"Then I shall make it weaker.\"\n\n\"And the bread was too sweet.\"\n\n\"Mara will be devastated.\"\n\nHe glanced toward the window seat.\n\nThen he chose a table near the back.\n\nIroh carried his tea there without comment.\n\nAfter closing, I found him washing the cups alone. I stood beside him and took a towel.\n\n\"Happy?\" I asked.\n\n\"Relieved.\"\n\n\"That he came back?\"\n\n\"That he chose to.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nIroh turned toward me, his hands damp from the washbasin.\n\n\"Thank you for not making his grief into a project.\"\n\n\"I almost did.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" he said gently. \"So did I.\"\n\nI leaned close enough to feel his breath against my cheek.\n\n\"Do you want company tonight?\"\n\nHe touched the back of my hand.\n\n\"Very much.\"\n\nThe kiss was soft and brief, a promise of nothing more than being together when we both wanted it.\n\nThat evening Iroh kept looking toward the spot where Halen had sat, but he did not tell Mina to hold it for him.\n\n“I could leave a note,” he said.\n\n“You could,” I answered. “Or you could wait until he asks for a place.”\n\nHe rubbed the rim of an empty cup with his thumb. The gesture reminded me of all the evenings he'd spent keeping a chair ready for someone who would never return. There had been Lu Ten. There had been the city he had once believed he could conquer. It was easier for him to prepare for a loss than to admit he couldn't arrange its ending.\n\nMina came over with the day's reservations. “The window table is requested tomorrow,” she told him. “Someone has been waiting a week for it.”\n\nIroh nodded. “Then let them have it.”\n\n“Are you sure?”\n\n“Mr. Halen is welcome wherever he wishes to sit. I would rather not turn his grief into a tenancy agreement.”\n\nShe gave him an approving look, then left us to finish locking up.\n\nOutside the shutters a wagon rolled over the stones and someone called for a child to come home. I brushed the flour from Iroh's sleeve and felt him lean into the touch. It was a small, tired movement. He would not need me to explain it.\n\n“Would you like me to stay?” I asked.\n\n“Yes,” he said. “Not because the chair is empty.”\n\nI kissed the corner of his mouth. He tasted faintly of citrus, the same blend he'd been serving all day, and his hand settled against my back without asking me to make tomorrow less uncertain.\n\n\nThe next morning, the chair by the window was occupied by a new customer who asked whether the house blend contained citrus.\n\nIt did.\n\nHalen sat at the back with his tea, reading a letter he had chosen to bring.\n\nNobody asked who it was from.","word_count":1708}},"love-and-duty":{"Love-And-Duty-12":{"number":12,"title":"Public Faces","content":"The first rumor appeared in a newspaper three days after our Tuesday conversation.\n\nAVATAR’S HOUSEHOLD EXPANDS?\n\nI stared at the headline while the tea beside my elbow went cold.\n\nThe article contained exactly two facts.\n\nAang had held my hand in public.\n\nToph had been seen leaving Katara’s clinic after dark.\n\nFrom this, the writer constructed a story involving secret ceremonies, political alliances, Air Nomad fertility law, and a sentence describing me as “an unidentified administrative companion.”\n\nI hated that phrase most.\n\nToph loved it.\n\nShe burst into my office carrying three copies.\n\n“Administrative Companion!”\n\n“Leave.”\n\n“Should I bow first?”\n\n“I will file you under municipal nuisance.”\n\nShe slapped the newspaper onto my desk. “They called me mysterious.”\n\n“They called you ‘heiress and metalbending eccentric.’”\n\n“Still good.”\n\nKatara arrived behind her looking far less amused.\n\nAang followed with the expression of a man trying very hard to practice his own philosophy about anger.\n\n“What do we do?” I asked.\n\nToph sat on the corner of my desk. “Nothing.”\n\nKatara said, “We correct the parts that could hurt people.”\n\nAang said, “I don’t want our relationship turned into policy.”\n\nThe room went quiet.\n\nThat was the danger beneath the ridiculous headline.\n\nAang was not merely a man in love. He was the Avatar. People interpreted his choices as statements even when he was choosing breakfast.\n\nKatara was a Water Tribe hero and respected healer.\n\nToph was a Beifong, inventor of metalbending, and increasingly public figure in city security.\n\nI had a desk.\n\nWhich made me, somehow, the easiest person to invent.\n\nThe article had already decided I represented nonbenders entering the Avatar’s inner circle.\n\nSome readers would see that as hopeful.\n\nOthers would see it as proof of corruption.\n\nNeither version had asked me who I was.\n\n“I don’t want to hide,” Katara said.\n\nToph shrugged. “Then don’t.”\n\n“I also don’t want strangers deciding they’re owed details.”\n\n“They’re not.”\n\nAang paced once across the office. “If we release a statement, it becomes official.”\n\n“It is official,” I said.\n\nHe stopped.\n\n“Our relationship is real whether a newspaper prints it or not.”\n\nAang looked at me.\n\n“That’s not what I meant.”\n\n“I know. But I think you’re still hoping there’s a version where your private life doesn’t become public because you’re sincere enough.”\n\nHis jaw tightened.\n\nKatara gave me a warning look.\n\nNot because I was wrong.\n\nBecause I had struck the bruise directly.\n\nAang took a breath.\n\n“You’re right.”\n\nToph’s head tilted.\n\nI waited for the rest.\n\n“I hate it,” he said.\n\nThere it was.\n\n“I spent my childhood with monks,” he continued. “People knew me, but not like this. Then I became the Avatar and suddenly everybody thought my life was a lesson.”\n\nKatara touched his arm.\n\nAang looked down at her hand.\n\n“I don’t want loving any of you to become a lesson.”\n\n“Then don’t teach one,” Toph said.\n\nWe all turned toward her.\n\nShe swung one foot lazily.\n\n“Say what’s true. Nothing else.”\n\nKatara considered that.\n\nI did too.\n\nAn hour later, we drafted the least interesting public statement in Cranefish Town history.\n\nAvatar Aang, Master Katara, Toph Beifong, and their partner confirmed that they were in a consensual relationship. They asked for privacy. Their personal relationship did not alter anyone’s public authority or responsibilities.\n\nThat was it.\n\nNo explanation of who kissed whom.\n\nNo declaration about marriage.\n\nNo Air Nation prophecy.\n\nNo “administrative companion.”\n\nToph wanted that phrase included as a joke.\n\nWe outvoted her.\n\nThe second problem was my name.\n\nThe newspapers already knew it from council records.\n\nUsing it publicly would make me easier to find.\n\nNot using it would turn me into a mystery people could fill with anything.\n\n“I want my name on it,” I said.\n\nAang looked uncertain. “Are you sure?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nKatara asked, “Do you want us to say anything about your work?”\n\n“No.”\n\nToph grinned. “Afraid they’ll find out how many drainage reports you’ve weaponized?”\n\n“I have never weaponized a drainage report.”\n\n“You hit a councilman with one.”\n\n“I gestured emphatically.”\n\n“With forty pages.”\n\nKatara laughed.\n\nThe statement went out that afternoon.\n\nBy evening, six newspapers had printed it.\n\nBy the next morning, street vendors were selling cheap sketches of the four of us that looked nothing like any of us.\n\nToph bought one.\n\n“Why?” I asked.\n\n“They gave me huge arms.”\n\n“They gave Aang a mustache.”\n\nAang looked offended. “I could grow one.”\n\nKatara stared at him.\n\n“No,” she said.\n\n“You don’t know that.”\n\n“I know enough.”\n\nThe jokes became less funny when a journalist waited outside the clinic and asked a woman carrying her sick child whether Katara's “unconventional household” affected patient care.\n\nKatara heard the question from inside. She stepped onto the threshold, sleeves rolled up and hands damp from washing them.\n\n“Her appointment is private,” she told him. “You may ask me about staffing or medicine. You will not use a patient to speculate about whom I love.”\n\nThe reporter protested that the public was entitled to know whether the Avatar's household compromised official decisions.\n\n“Then request the clinic's public accounts,” she said. “You'll find the hours I've worked there and every supply purchase that has gone through the ledger. You will not find a mother's name.”\n\nI watched the woman's shoulders drop when Katara closed the door behind her.\n\nAang wanted to confront the newspaper's owner immediately. Toph wanted to know whether their printing press had ever met a boulder. Both suggestions had obvious appeal and equally obvious problems.\n\n“I could issue a statement,” Aang said.\n\n“You can,” Katara answered. “But don't speak for my patients, and don't promise what you haven't asked me about.”\n\nHe sat down beside the clinic steps and thought. For the first time that afternoon he looked less like the Avatar everyone had opinions about and more like a man realizing that being beloved didn't make him the spokesman for everybody who loved him.\n\nToph tapped the stone once with her heel. “You want my opinion, Twinkletoes?”\n\n“Always.”\n\n“Then say you're dating people. Don't turn it into a public service announcement on the meaning of love. And leave my name out unless I give it to you.”\n\nI took out a clean sheet. We wrote down what each of us was actually willing to make public. It was far shorter than the editor's story.\n\n\nThe public reaction was not entirely funny.\n\nA letter arrived at the council office accusing me of distracting the Avatar from his duty.\n\nAnother called Katara immoral.\n\nA third praised Toph for “refusing conventional domesticity,” which made her mutter that strangers were somehow annoying even when they agreed with her.\n\nOne letter asked whether Aang planned to restore Air Nomad polygamy.\n\nHe read it twice.\n\n“I don’t know if that was ever a thing.”\n\n“Does it matter?” I asked.\n\n“It matters if people are inventing my culture to explain me.”\n\nThat one hurt him.\n\nI could see it.\n\nHe spent the afternoon in the temple room he had built above the house, meditating beneath old Air Nomad carvings. I did not follow immediately.\n\nKatara did not either.\n\n“Give him a little time,” she said.\n\nToph’s foot tapped the floor.\n\n“He hates when dead people get turned into costumes.”\n\nThe bluntness was pure Toph.\n\nIt was also exactly right.\n\nWhen I finally climbed the stairs, Aang was seated before a small statue of Gyatso.\n\nI sat near the door.\n\nHe opened his eyes.\n\n“I’m sorry,” I said.\n\n“For what?”\n\n“For the world being terrible at boundaries.”\n\nAang smiled faintly.\n\n“That isn’t yours to apologize for.”\n\n“I know. I’m practicing Katara.”\n\nHe laughed.\n\nI moved closer.\n\n“Do you regret saying it publicly?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Do you regret us?”\n\nAang’s expression sharpened.\n\n“No.”\n\nThe answer was immediate enough to settle something in me.\n\nHe held out his hand.\n\nI took it.\n\n“We’re going to get things wrong,” he said.\n\n“Obviously. Toph is involved.”\n\nFrom downstairs came a muffled shout: “I heard that!”\n\nAang laughed so hard he lost his meditative posture.\n\nKatara called up, “Dinner!”\n\nAang squeezed my hand.\n\nWe went downstairs together.\n\nThe world knew now.\n\nNot the private shape of us.\n\nNot the things that belonged only to four people around a table.\n\nBut enough.\n\nAt dinner, Toph propped the terrible newspaper sketch against the teapot.\n\nAang’s drawn mustache curled nearly to his ears.\n\nKatara had been given a crown.\n\nToph looked like she could bench-press a badgermole.\n\nI had been drawn holding a scroll.\n\n“Honestly,” Toph said, “they nailed Paperwork.”\n\nI threw a dumpling at her.\n\nShe caught it in her mouth.\n\nPublic life could have the headline.\n\nIt could not have the way Aang’s knee pressed against mine under the table, or the way Katara automatically split the last sweet bun into four pieces, or the way Toph slid the largest piece onto my plate when she thought no one noticed.\n\nThose things remained ours.\n\nThe newspaper did not stop printing stories because we refused to feed them.\n\nFor a week, strangers developed opinions about which of us Aang “really” loved, whether Katara was secretly heartbroken, whether Toph had coerced anyone, and—my personal favorite—whether I was an elaborate political symbol invented by the council.\n\n“I knew you were suspicious,” Toph said when I read that one aloud.\n\n“I’m devastated to learn I don’t exist.”\n\nKatara folded the paper into a neat square. “Stop reading them.”\n\n“You read them.”\n\n“I read them so I know what people are saying.”\n\n“That is different because?”\n\n“Because I said it more confidently.”\n\nAang laughed into his sleeve.\n\nThe humor helped, but not enough.\n\nThat afternoon, a woman stopped me outside the public records office and asked which of them I preferred.\n\nI stared at her.\n\nShe waited as if this were reasonable.\n\n“Do you ask parents which child is real?” I said finally.\n\nHer face changed.\n\nI regretted the comparison immediately—relationships were not children, and love did not become moral simply because there was more than one person involved—but the question had done what I needed. It interrupted her certainty.\n\nWhen I told the others later, Katara said, “You don’t owe strangers a lesson every time they’re rude.”\n\nToph added, “Sometimes you can just say none of your business.”\n\nAang considered this. “Could I say that as the Avatar?”\n\n“Yes,” three of us answered.\n\nHe looked delighted.\n\nThe next headline called him evasive.\n\nToph framed it.\n\nThe framed headline eventually hung in the hallway. Beneath it, I added a second clipping: AVATAR DECLINES TO COMMENT ON PRIVATE HOUSEHOLD MATTERS.\n\nToph declared it an improvement.\n\nAang looked absurdly proud of himself.\n\nKatara said nothing, but I caught her smiling every time she passed it.\n\nEventually the hallway wall became a joke only we understood: evidence that public certainty could be loud, wrong, and still less important than what happened at our table.\n\nThat mattered more than anything they printed.\n","word_count":1812},"Love-And-Duty-25":{"number":25,"title":"South","content":"The Southern Water Tribe appeared first as a line of blue against white.\n\nKatara saw it before I did.\n\nShe had been standing near Appa’s head for the last hour, one hand resting against his saddle, eyes fixed on the horizon with a stillness I had learned not to interrupt.\n\nThen her breath changed.\n\n“There.”\n\nAang moved beside her.\n\nToph, wrapped in enough fur to qualify as architecture, muttered, “Describe.”\n\nI leaned over the saddle edge.\n\n“Buildings. More than I expected. Blue roofs. Ice walls. Ships in the bay.”\n\nKatara smiled.\n\nIt was not uncomplicated.\n\nI could see that too.\n\nShe had told me stories of a village small enough that everyone knew who had sneezed before breakfast. Snow huts. A few tents. Children running between them. Her grandmother’s voice. Sokka trying to train six-year-olds as soldiers because all the adults had gone to war.\n\nBelow us was a city.\n\nNot Cranefish Town large.\n\nBut transformed.\n\nStone foundations. Timber halls. Water channels. Northern-style towers beside older southern structures. Work crews extending a harbor wall while ships unloaded lumber and grain.\n\nKatara’s home had survived.\n\nSurvival had changed it.\n\nAppa landed outside the main settlement in a storm of snow.\n\nSokka reached us before the snow settled.\n\nHe hit Aang first with a hug disguised as a tackle.\n\nThen Katara.\n\nThen Toph, who bent a ridge of ice-crusted earth under his feet and made him stumble.\n\n“Missed you too, Snoozles.”\n\nSokka looked at me.\n\n“Administrative Companion.”\n\nI stared.\n\nToph betrayed me with immediate laughter.\n\n“You told him?”\n\n“I wrote him.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“History.”\n\nSokka held out both arms.\n\nI stepped into the hug.\n\n“Welcome south,” he said. “We have snow, fish, political arguments, and several buildings my sister has opinions about.”\n\nKatara glared.\n\n“I just got here.”\n\n“You had opinions from the sky.”\n\n“I did not.”\n\n“You made the face.”\n\nToph pointed at him. “See? Other people can hear faces.”\n\nGran Gran was waiting at the family home.\n\nAge had made her smaller than I expected and somehow no less formidable. She hugged Katara for a long time, held Aang’s face between both hands, slapped Sokka lightly for hovering, and greeted Toph by saying, “Still barefoot?”\n\nToph looked delighted.\n\nThen Gran Gran turned toward me.\n\nThere are moments when being the newest person in a family feels like standing in a doorway while everyone decides whether it opens.\n\nGran Gran looked me up and down.\n\nNot assessing my body.\n\nAssessing my nerves.\n\nThen she said, “You’re the one who works with papers.”\n\nToph made a strangled noise.\n\nI closed my eyes.\n\n“I’m going home.”\n\nGran Gran laughed and pulled me into a hug.\n\nThe door opened.\n\nDinner lasted three hours.\n\nHakoda joined us halfway through, windburned from a council trip. He greeted Katara with quiet affection that made her suddenly look younger. Not childish. Just somebody’s daughter.\n\nI had not seen that version of her before.\n\nIt felt intimate in a way kissing had not prepared me for.\n\nWe ate sea-prune stew, roasted roots for Aang, fish for everyone who wanted it, and a sweet frozen berry dish Toph complained had “the texture of betrayal.”\n\nConversation moved around me like water.\n\nOld names. Cousins. Repairs. Harbor politics. Sokka’s latest engineering problem. Who had married whom. Who had fallen through ice and blamed a seal.\n\nI understood half of it.\n\nNobody stopped every thirty seconds to explain.\n\nI liked that.\n\nBelonging did not require becoming the center of someone else’s history.\n\nAfter dinner, Katara asked if I wanted to walk.\n\nJust us.\n\nWe followed a packed path beyond the newer buildings toward the shoreline.\n\nThe cold was enormous.\n\nIt had personality.\n\nIt entered my boots, found every gap in my coat, and appeared personally offended that I had bones.\n\nKatara moved through it as if the wind were an old relative.\n\n“Still want to walk?” she asked.\n\n“I’m committed now.”\n\n“That’s not what I asked.”\n\nI looked at her.\n\nShe smiled.\n\nI sighed. “Five more minutes.”\n\n“Better.”\n\nWe stopped near a low ridge overlooking the water.\n\nKatara looked back at the settlement.\n\n“This used to be empty.”\n\nI waited.\n\n“Not empty empty. There were houses. But not… that.”\n\nA crane turned slowly near the harbor.\n\n“Do you hate it?”\n\n“No.”\n\nThe answer came too quickly.\n\nKatara heard it herself.\n\nShe pulled her hood tighter.\n\n“Sometimes.”\n\nThat was more honest.\n\n“I hated the North for a while,” she said. “Not the people. The way they looked at us like the South was a broken copy of them.”\n\nI remembered stories of her first waterbending master refusing to teach women combat. Of customs she had challenged because tradition, to Katara, was never an excuse for injustice.\n\n“Then I came home after the war and Northern builders were everywhere. New walls. New canals. New clothes. People kept saying progress.”\n\nShe scraped one boot through snow.\n\n“And part of me wanted to scream that we had survived a hundred years. We didn’t need to become somebody else to prove we survived correctly.”\n\nThe wind tore at the words.\n\n“What changed?” I asked.\n\n“Nothing all at once.”\n\nVery Katara answer.\n\nShe looked toward the harbor lights.\n\n“I started asking who was choosing the changes. Whether local people had power. Whether new systems actually helped. Some did. Some didn’t.”\n\nShe smiled faintly.\n\n“I learned nostalgia can protect things. It can also pretend the past was kinder than it was.”\n\nI thought about Aang rebuilding festivals. Toph reading a letter from her father. Cranefish Town arguing about whether mixed neighborhoods were a corruption or a future.\n\n“Home again,” I said.\n\nKatara glanced at me.\n\n“Apparently we have one theme.”\n\n“Toph will hate that.”\n\n“Good.”\n\nWe stood in silence.\n\nThen Katara’s expression changed.\n\n“What?” I asked.\n\nShe pointed.\n\nFarther down the shore, a group of children had gathered around a cut in the ice. One was kneeling too close.\n\nKatara was moving before I understood.\n\nThe ice broke.\n\nA child disappeared.\n\nKatara ran.\n\nI followed badly.\n\nWater exploded from the opening in a smooth column. The child rose inside it, coughing and terrified, carried toward Katara’s arms. She dropped to her knees, wrapped them in her own coat, and pressed glowing hands against their chest.\n\nThe other children screamed for adults.\n\nI arrived breathless.\n\n“Blankets,” Katara said.\n\nI ran back toward the nearest house.\n\nBy the time Hakoda and half the neighborhood arrived, the child was conscious and furious about being carried.\n\nKatara laughed with tears in her eyes.\n\nThe child's mother came to the door before the fire had fully warmed Katara's hands. She brought an empty wooden bucket and a frightened expression.\n\n“I don't know what to say.”\n\nKatara stood, but Gran Gran stopped her with a look. “Sit down. You have done enough for the moment.”\n\nThe woman set the bucket on the floor. “The warning stakes were gone after the last storm. We told the watch, but no one came.”\n\nHakoda's face hardened. He went to fetch the shore patrol ledger while Katara asked how long the gap had been open. Toph pressed her feet against the floorboards, listening to the movement outside. She couldn't tell whether the ice would hold from inside a house, and she didn't pretend she could.\n\nAang offered to mark a temporary safer route while the patrol checked the whole shoreline. Katara accepted, but she insisted the child's mother help decide where the signs belonged. A path that kept visitors safe while cutting local families off from their nets would not be a fair solution.\n\n“I'm not staying here to run the patrol,” Katara said quietly afterward. “I want to help, but if they need me every day, somebody needs to ask the community whether that's what *they* want.”\n\nGran Gran handed her a fresh cup. “You have come home, child. You haven't come back to become the whole village.”\n\nI watched Katara's shoulders lower. She loved this place so fiercely that she could mistake being indispensable for belonging to it. The rest of us could carry blankets and messages. Only she could decide what obligations she was willing to take home.\n\n\nLater, back at the house, she sat beside the fire in dry clothes while Gran Gran lectured everyone about shoreline safety as if Katara herself had fallen in.\n\nToph sat on the floor beside Katara’s chair, pretending her proximity meant nothing.\n\nAang brought tea.\n\nI tucked a blanket over Katara’s knees.\n\nShe looked at me.\n\n“This part hasn’t changed,” she said.\n\n“What part?”\n\n“Everybody knowing before you get home.”\n\nFrom the kitchen, Sokka shouted, “The whole south knows!”\n\nKatara groaned.\n\nI laughed.\n\nOutside, the transformed village kept building itself beneath old stars.\n\nKatara’s home was not preserved in ice.\n\nNeither was she.\n\nMaybe loving where you came from meant allowing it to live long enough to become unfamiliar—and staying brave enough to keep asking whether the change still belonged to the people who called it home.\n\nThat night, Katara took me beyond the newest buildings to where the snow still swallowed sound.\n\nShe carried a waterskin at her hip but did not bend from it. Instead she knelt, pressed her palm to the ground, and drew a ribbon of snow upward until it melted between her fingers.\n\n“This is the first place I taught myself,” she said.\n\nI waited.\n\n“Not this exact spot. Everything looks different.”\n\nThe confession was quieter than the wind.\n\nShe shaped the water into a small sphere, divided it into two, joined it again.\n\n“I used to think coming home would feel like returning to something that had been waiting for me.”\n\n“Did it?”\n\n“Some parts.”\n\nShe looked toward the lights of the settlement. New roofs. Wider paths. Structures built with materials that had not been common here when she was a child.\n\n“Other parts feel like arriving late to my own life.”\n\nI moved closer but did not touch her until she reached for my hand.\n\n“I’m happy they have more,” she said. “I wanted this. Food. healers. Trade. Choices. I just—”\n\n“Miss what had to change for them to get it?”\n\nHer fingers tightened around mine.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThere was no clever answer.\n\nAang would have understood loss shaped by absence. Toph would have understood home shaped by leaving. I understood only enough to stay beside her.\n\nKatara bent the water into a thin arc and let it freeze into a clear bridge between two stones.\n\n“Pretty,” I said.\n\n“It won’t last.”\n\n“Still pretty.”\n\nShe smiled.\n\nBy morning the bridge had softened at the edges.\n\nBy afternoon it was water again.\n\nNothing about that made it less real while it held.\n\nChange had held.\n\nSo had we.\n","word_count":1773},"love-and-duty-47":{"number":47,"title":"The Longlight Kites","content":"The festival committee wanted Aang to teach everybody an Air Nomad sacred ceremony.\n\nThat was how the request reached us.\n\nPip had written a polite letter asking whether the Avatar might \"share the beautiful flying traditions of his people\" at the Longlight Kite Festival. They hadn't intended harm. The committee liked bright fabrics, impressive demonstrations, and the idea that one famous person could make a local celebration feel important.\n\nAang read the letter twice.\n\nThen he set it down.\n\n\"That's not what kites are for,\" he said.\n\nToph leaned across the table.\n\n\"Usually they're for flying.\"\n\nKatara hid a smile.\n\nAang looked at her.\n\n\"Don't encourage her.\"\n\n\"I'm not.\"\n\n\"You are.\"\n\nI took the letter.\n\n\"What's wrong with the request?\"\n\nHe rubbed the back of his neck.\n\n\"Some Air Nomad practices are meant to be taught in specific ways, by people who understand their meaning. I can show children how to make a kite. I can't turn every tradition I remember into entertainment for a festival.\"\n\nThe words were quiet, but there was grief beneath them.\n\nThe Longlight Festival belonged to Lanternweald, not to the Air Nomads. It had begun when the valley's stewards needed visible wind markers along the ridges before the winter storms. Families made bright cloth shapes and attached them to safe poles so travelers could see where the gusts were strongest. Over the years the wind markers became kites. Some people decorated them with symbols from home, but nobody had a claim on anyone else's sacred practices.\n\nThe committee had forgotten the difference between sharing a skill and borrowing a culture.\n\n\"Then we tell them,\" I said.\n\nAang nodded.\n\n\"I'll teach kite-making. But not a ceremony that isn't mine to give away.\"\n\nWe went to the meeting together.\n\nPip looked mortified when Aang explained.\n\n\"I didn't mean to make a spectacle of your people,\" they said.\n\n\"I know,\" Aang answered. \"That's why I'm telling you.\"\n\nThe committee adjusted the program. The opening would explain the valley's own history of wind markers and safe travel routes. People could bring designs meaningful to them, but no one would be asked to display sacred symbols or explain private beliefs. Aang would teach balance, frame strength, and how to test a kite without endangering bystanders.\n\nTamsin approved the load limits. Fen marked the ridge where the wind was steady enough. Edda inspected the path to the viewing platform, and Danse offered to help secure the temporary railings.\n\nToph was suspicious.\n\n\"Are we doing another festival where the whole town thinks wood is indestructible?\"\n\n\"Not this time,\" I said.\n\n\"Good. My patience has limits.\"\n\nBy the next morning the square was full of frames and colored cloth.\n\nAang sat on the floor with six children and two adults, teaching them how to tie a bridle knot. He was patient when the first kite twisted sideways. He didn't take it away and fix it. He asked which part felt uneven and let the child examine the string.\n\nI thought of Lio's apprenticeship at Tideglass.\n\nThe lesson was spreading.\n\nKatara helped a family choose lighter fabric because their child couldn't hold a heavy frame for long. Toph tested the stones beneath the launching area and declared one section too loose for running. We marked it off.\n\nI made a kite with a pattern of small amber circles.\n\n\"Very original,\" Toph said.\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"It looks like a potato.\"\n\n\"That's cultural criticism, and I reject it.\"\n\nShe laughed.\n\nAang looked over.\n\n\"I like it.\"\n\n\"You would,\" Toph said. \"You like anything that flies.\"\n\n\"That's not true.\"\n\nShe raised an eyebrow.\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"All right. Mostly true.\"\n\nThe first launch went badly.\n\nMy kite rose, tilted left, and fell into a patch of soft grass. The string tangled around my wrist.\n\nAang came toward me.\n\n\"Need help?\"\n\n\"Yes. I need you to stop looking so pleased with yourself.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\n\"That's not a repair.\"\n\n\"Can you help with the string?\"\n\nHe sat beside me and showed me how to adjust the balance point. He didn't make the knot for me. It took three tries.\n\nWhen the kite finally rose, the cloth caught the light. The amber circles shone against the sky.\n\nI felt absurdly happy.\n\nAang watched it.\n\n\"That's beautiful,\" he said.\n\n\"It's a potato.\"\n\n\"A very graceful potato.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nAt the materials table, a boy named Lio arrived with a frame too heavy for the string he'd brought.\n\n\"I wanted it to be bigger than Maui's,\" he admitted.\n\nToph folded her arms.\n\n\"Then you wanted a kite or a monument?\"\n\nHe looked offended.\n\n\"Both.\"\n\nKatara knelt beside the frame and asked him to lift it as he would when the wind caught. He managed it for three seconds before his arms shook.\n\n\"Would you like to make a lighter version?\" she asked.\n\nLio looked toward Maui, who had been asked to hold a line at the far end of the field.\n\n\"Will he laugh?\"\n\n\"Absolutely,\" Toph said. \"Then we'll laugh at his when it falls.\"\n\nAang sat with us and showed Lio how to trim the crossbar without weakening the center joint. He didn't take over the design. Lio chose a smaller sail, kept the original painted symbol, and wrote down the dimensions in his notebook so he could teach the next person.\n\nWhen the kite finally lifted, Maui cheered so loudly that two others looked around.\n\nLio rolled his eyes. Then he grinned.\n\nI thought about the apprentices at Brasswatch and the first boats at Tideglass. Skill was traveling between the districts without requiring everybody to become the same sort of person.\n\nA volunteer came by to ask whether the event would run another hour. The sky was still bright, but Fen's wind board showed stronger gusts expected near dusk.\n\nAang checked the reading and shook his head.\n\n\"We close launches at the posted time. People can stay for food and music if they like.\"\n\nHe looked disappointed. He loved seeing the children fly, and I knew how much the afternoon meant to him.\n\n\"Are you okay?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes,\" he said, then corrected himself. \"I'm sad to stop. That doesn't mean the rule is wrong.\"\n\nToph made a satisfied noise.\n\n\"Finally. An Avatar who understands weather isn't impressed by fame.\"\n\nHe stuck his tongue out at her.\n\nI laughed, then helped roll the unused string for the next festival.\n\nThe festival continued through the afternoon. Some kites never flew. Some tore in the gusts. One child painted a fierce red bird that spun in circles before landing directly on Maui's head.\n\nHe declared it an act of war.\n\nNobody believed him.\n\nA gust snapped one of the western marker lines. The kite spun sideways, its cord whipping against the post.\n\n“Everybody back,” Katara called, moving the children behind the safety rope.\n\nToph planted both feet and felt the post through the stone. “Anchor's loose. Not the whole ridge. Stop panicking about the whole ridge.”\n\nAang could have caught the kite in a column of air. He looked at the children under the line and decided against it.\n\n“We let it come down,” he said. “Nobody stands under a snapping cord for a show.”\n\nPip asked whether they should cancel the festival. Katara asked for the wind readings and the injury report. Toph asked who had inspected the anchor that morning. Aang listened without treating his title as a substitute for answers.\n\n“Close the west side,” he said. “The stewards can decide if the east markers stay up.”\n\n“And if they don't?” I asked.\n\n“Then everybody gets very good at folding kites.”\n\nWe recovered the fabric after the post was secured. Nobody had to be the hero of the incident; someone had missed a weak anchor, and others had noticed in time.\n\n“Would the Air Nomads have laughed at the potato kite?” I asked.\n\n“Absolutely,” Aang said, and his smile carried more than one feeling. For a moment I could hear delight and loss together, without trying to make either one disappear.\n\n\nNear sunset, Aang went to the edge of the ridge alone. I followed, leaving enough space for him to turn back if he wanted quiet.\n\n\"Mind company?\" I asked.\n\nHe shook his head.\n\nThe sky was pale gold. Kites moved above the valley like small living shapes, each with a different line and angle. The old Threadway arches glowed below us, not as trophies but as paths people could choose.\n\n\"I miss them,\" Aang said.\n\nI didn't need to ask who.\n\nHe looked at the kites.\n\n\"When people ask me to perform the traditions of the Air Nomads, part of me wants to say yes. I want them to know how beautiful my people were. I want the world to remember.\"\n\n\"And the other part?\"\n\n\"Doesn't want their memory reduced to a show.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"You get to decide what you share.\"\n\n\"Sometimes I feel selfish.\"\n\n\"Preserving meaning isn't selfish.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"You sound like Katara.\"\n\n\"High compliment.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\nWe stood together while the first evening lanterns came on.\n\n\"Can I ask you something?\" he said.\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\n\"Do you ever feel pulled between everyone you love?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"How do you know where you belong?\"\n\nI thought about the calendar at Riverstone, the quiet nights at Jasmine Steps, the workshop lights at Brasswatch, and the sea-glass orchard at Tideglass. I thought of Katara coming home, Toph choosing her own time, and Aang standing beside me with a history I could respect but never replace.\n\n\"I don't think belonging is a single place,\" I said. \"I think it's the choices we keep making honestly.\"\n\nHe leaned close.\n\n\"That's a good answer.\"\n\n\"Don't let Toph hear you.\"\n\n\"She'll say it's a terrible answer.\"\n\n\"Then she'll steal it.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nI kissed him under the first star, gentle and unhurried. The wind tugged at my hair and carried the sound of the festival up the ridge.\n\nWhen the wind calmed, the committee discovered that the largest kite had torn its lower seam. One of the volunteers suggested keeping it on display with Aang's name painted across the frame, as though the Avatar had made it himself.\n\nAang looked appalled. “I didn't.”\n\n“We thought it would raise donations,” the volunteer said.\n\nToph ran two fingers along the frame. “Then put the maker's name on it. Unless the maker is a mysterious enchanted knot with a celebrity agent.”\n\nPip checked the lending list. The kite had been built by two young apprentices using canvas borrowed from a Tideglass sail shop. One of them had agreed to a public demonstration, not to surrender their work or be replaced by a famous signature.\n\nKatara offered to help write an accurate sign explaining who had constructed it and why the flying test stopped when the cord began to fray. The volunteer looked disappointed. An almost-spectacular failure was not as marketable as a legend about the Avatar.\n\nAang knelt beside the damaged seam. “Would the makers like help repairing it?”\n\n“Ask them,” Pip replied.\n\nHe did. One wanted to work with him; the other preferred Toph's advice about the frame. Toph made an exaggerated show of being honored and then spent twenty minutes listening while the apprentice explained their own design.\n\nI watched Aang carry the repair box without being asked to bless anything. He looked happier when the second child managed the knot without him than he had when the crowd had shouted his name.\n\nThe festival belonged to the valley. The Air Nomad stories he chose to share still belonged to his people. Neither fact diminished the other.\n\n\nLater we packed away the kites that could be reused and labeled the damaged ones for repair. Aang helped without being asked to perform anything sacred.\n\nAt home, Katara had made tea. Toph was complaining about the potato kite.\n\nI hung it by the window.\n\nIt looked ridiculous.\n\nIt was mine.","word_count":1977}}};for(const s of lib.series){const entries=changes[s.series_slug]||{};for(const ch of s.chapters){const e=entries[ch.id];if(e&&e.number===ch.chapter_number){ch.title=e.title;ch.content=e.content;ch.word_count=e.word_count;}}}})();
